**Both are techniques for making a composite widget (toolbar, tab list, listbox, menu, grid) a single Tab stop with arrow-key navigation inside. Roving tabindex moves real DOM focus: one item has `tabindex="0"`, the rest `-1`, and arrow keys shift that and call `focus()`. `aria-activedescendant` keeps DOM focus on a container or input and points to the "virtually focused" child by id.**

## Why composites are one Tab stop
A toolbar with 15 buttons should not cost 15 Tab presses to pass. The APG keyboard guidance: Tab moves between widgets, arrows move within a widget, Home/End jump to the ends, and Tab back into the widget returns to the last active item.

## Roving tabindex
```tsx
function TabList({ tabs, selected, onSelect }: { tabs: string[]; selected: number; onSelect: (i: number) => void }) {
  const refs = useRef<(HTMLButtonElement | null)[]>([]);
  const go = (i: number) => {
    const n = (i + tabs.length) % tabs.length;
    onSelect(n);                    // automatic activation
    refs.current[n]?.focus();
  };
  return (
    <div role="tablist" aria-label="Settings"
      onKeyDown={(e) => {
        const map: Record<string, number> = { ArrowRight: selected + 1, ArrowLeft: selected - 1, Home: 0, End: tabs.length - 1 };
        if (e.key in map) { e.preventDefault(); go(map[e.key]); }
      }}>
      {tabs.map((t, i) => (
        <button key={t} ref={(el) => { refs.current[i] = el; }} role="tab"
          aria-selected={i === selected} tabIndex={i === selected ? 0 : -1}>
          {t}
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```
Pros: real focus means `:focus-visible` styles, `scrollIntoView`, and screen reader focus tracking all work natively. Cons: you manage tabindex on every item and must handle items being added or removed.

## aria-activedescendant
```text
<input role="combobox" aria-controls="lb" aria-expanded="true"
       aria-activedescendant="opt-2">
<ul role="listbox" id="lb">
  <li role="option" id="opt-1">Berlin</li>
  <li role="option" id="opt-2" aria-selected="true">Bern</li>
```
DOM focus never leaves the input, so typing continues to work. On each arrow key you update the attribute; the screen reader announces the referenced option as if focused. You must: scroll the active option into view yourself, style it (there is no `:focus` on it), keep ids stable and rendered, and only reference descendants or elements the focused element controls.

## Choosing
| Use | Technique |
| --- | --- |
| Combobox / autocomplete (typing must continue) | aria-activedescendant |
| Toolbar, tabs, radio group, menu, tree, grid | roving tabindex (most libraries) |
| Huge virtualised lists | aria-activedescendant can avoid focusing unmounted nodes, but the active option must be rendered |

Support for `aria-activedescendant` has historically been less consistent across screen readers (especially VoiceOver), which is another reason roving tabindex is the default outside comboboxes. React Aria, Radix and Ariakit implement both, which is a strong argument for using them for complex composites.

**In an SPA, client-side navigation swaps content without a page load, so screen readers announce nothing and focus stays on a link that may no longer exist. On each route change, update `document.title` and either move focus to the new page's main heading (with `tabindex="-1"`) or announce the new page title in a polite live region. Pick one strategy and apply it consistently in the router layer.**

## What a full page load gives you for free
- Focus resets to the top of the document.
- The screen reader announces the new title and starts reading.
- The next Tab starts from the beginning.
A client-side transition loses all three.

## Strategy 1: focus the heading
```tsx
function RouteFocus() {
  const pathname = usePathname();
  const first = useRef(true);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (first.current) { first.current = false; return; } // not on initial load
    document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("main h1")?.focus();
  }, [pathname]);
  return null;
}
// Each page: <h1 tabIndex={-1}>Invoices</h1>
```
The heading text is announced, and the next Tab continues in the new content. Marcy Sutton's 2019 user testing of client-side routing with disabled users (published on the Gatsby blog) compared several focus and announcement techniques and is the best primary source on this question; the common thread was that users need both a focus position in the new content and a clear announcement of what changed. Hide the focus ring on the heading with care: it is not interactive, but keyboard users still benefit from seeing where they are.

## Strategy 2: announce
A visually hidden `aria-live="polite"` region that receives "Invoices" (or the full title) after each navigation, while focus goes to a stable element such as the top of the page or a skip link. Next.js does this out of the box: its route announcer reads `document.title`, falling back to the first `h1`, then the pathname. That makes **unique, descriptive page titles** (via the Metadata API) the most important accessibility input in a Next.js app.

Doing both strategies can produce double announcements; choose one.

## When not to move focus
- **Initial load**: the browser handles it.
- **Query-string changes on the same page** (filters, sorting, tabs synced to the URL): keep focus on the control the user used and announce the result ("24 results").
- **Hash links**: let the browser scroll; ensure the target is focusable (`tabindex="-1"`).

## Related focus-loss cases
Deleted items, closed popovers, replaced forms and unmounted virtualised rows all drop focus to `body`. Check with `document.activeElement` in the console after the action, and in Playwright with `expect(locator).toBeFocused()`.

**Honour the operating system's reduce-motion setting through the `prefers-reduced-motion` media query in CSS and `matchMedia` in JavaScript, replacing large movement (translation, zoom, parallax, spinning) with subtle fades or instant changes. Separately, never flash more than three times per second, and give anything that auto-moves for more than five seconds a pause control.**

## Why it matters
People with vestibular disorders can experience dizziness, nausea and migraines from large or parallax motion; some people with attention disorders find motion distracting; flashing content can trigger seizures in people with photosensitive epilepsy.

## Relevant criteria
- **2.3.1 Three Flashes or Below Threshold (A)**: seizure safety; not a preference.
- **2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide (A)**: auto-moving, blinking, scrolling or auto-updating content that lasts more than five seconds and appears alongside other content needs a pause, stop or hide mechanism (carousels, marquees, background videos, animated hero sections).
- **2.3.3 Animation from Interactions (AAA)**: interaction-triggered motion can be disabled.
- **1.4.2 Audio Control (A)**: auto-playing audio over three seconds needs a stop or volume control.

## CSS
```css
.panel {
  transition: transform 300ms ease-out, opacity 300ms ease-out;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .panel { transition: opacity 150ms linear; transform: none; }
  html { scroll-behavior: auto; }
  .parallax { background-attachment: scroll; }
}
```
Or invert the default and opt into motion: put animations inside `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)` so the unanimated version is the baseline.

## JavaScript
```ts
const query = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
let reduce = query.matches;
query.addEventListener("change", (e) => { reduce = e.matches; }); // user can toggle live
```
Wrap it in a hook (`usePrefersReducedMotion`) and branch animation parameters. Josh Comeau's article on accessible animations in React walks through this hook and the opt-in approach. Animation libraries need explicit handling: Framer Motion has a `MotionConfig reducedMotion="user"` option, GSAP has `gsap.matchMedia()`; smooth-scroll libraries and canvas or WebGL animations need manual checks.

## Reduced is not none
Keep feedback that helps: opacity fades, colour changes, small progress indicators. Remove spatial movement and anything that fills the screen. Removing all transitions can make state changes harder to notice.

## Other controls
- Carousels: no auto-advance, or a visible pause button that is keyboard reachable and stops on focus or hover.
- Background video: pause control, and do not autoplay when reduced motion is set.
- Offer an in-app motion setting for users who do not know about the OS setting.

## Testing
Chrome DevTools Rendering panel emulates `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`; check each animated component in both modes.

**Testing Library's guiding principle is that tests should resemble how users use the software, so its query priority puts `getByRole` (role plus accessible name) first, then `getByLabelText`. Those queries read the same information assistive technology uses, so if a test cannot find "button named Save", a screen reader user probably cannot either. Every component test doubles as a basic accessibility test.**

## The priority list
```text
1. getByRole("button", { name: "Save" })   accessible to everyone
2. getByLabelText("Email address")         form fields
3. getByPlaceholderText, getByText, getByDisplayValue
4. getByAltText, getByTitle                 semantic queries
5. getByTestId                              escape hatch
```

## Example
```tsx
test("toggle exposes pressed state", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<MuteButton />);
  const btn = screen.getByRole("button", { name: "Mute" });
  expect(btn).toHaveAttribute("aria-pressed", "false");
  await user.click(btn);
  expect(btn).toHaveAttribute("aria-pressed", "true");
});

test("error is associated with the field", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<SignupForm />);
  await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: "Create account" }));
  const email = screen.getByRole("textbox", { name: "Email address" });
  expect(email).toBeInvalid();
  expect(email).toHaveAccessibleDescription(/enter your email/i);
});
```
If someone refactors the button into a `div` or deletes the label association, these tests fail. A `getByTestId("save-btn")` test keeps passing while the control becomes unusable.

## Useful options and matchers
- `name` (string or regex) matches the computed accessible name; `description` matches the description.
- `level` for headings: `getByRole("heading", { level: 2, name: "Billing" })`.
- `checked`, `pressed`, `expanded`, `selected` filter by state.
- `hidden: true` includes elements excluded from the tree (useful for debugging).
- `toHaveAccessibleName`, `toHaveAccessibleDescription`, `toBeInvalid`, `toHaveFocus` from jest-dom.
- `logRoles(container)` prints the roles and names Testing Library sees.

## Limits
jsdom approximates the accessibility tree; it does not do layout, so visibility and contrast are not real, and computed roles can differ slightly from browsers. Pair with axe in a real browser and manual screen reader checks.

## When test ids are fine
Elements with no accessible role or text that the user still perceives (a canvas, a chart container), or where text is dynamic and not the point of the test. They should be the exception.

Playwright follows the same philosophy: `page.getByRole("button", { name: "Pay" })` and `getByLabel` are its recommended locators, and they are resilient to markup changes that do not affect users. Kent C. Dodds' "Common mistakes with React Testing Library" covers these choices in detail.
